title bakery round ten -- the receipt-key options written for the ruling, no build
prompt Baton lap for BAKERY on the grain-bakery tree: read the inner prompt and the card, pick the most durable solvable keystone, log it, end with one closing line.
think The inner prompt's fruit is one shared build cache and one shared cold-run cache across eight checkouts. The card names the receipt key as the blocking seam: tree_digest in tools/fixtures/s/standing_equipment_run.sh opens with git rev-parse HEAD, so one tree at two commits reads two digests.
think Changing that key changes the roster runner's contract. The card reserves that for Keaton's ruling (YOURS line, ITINERARY section on the receipt key). Building before the ruling would spend the contract on a guess.
think Bakery round nine already holds on this same ruling (20261010.020227). Another identical hold adds a row and no decision, so this round writes the options out in one place for the ruling to pick from.
think The claim board is empty (construction/fleet-claims.kyri has no live claim), and tools/fixtures/p/path_absence_scan.sh reads both instrument paths present upstream with commits_behind=2, so no peer is building the same thing.
think No code, witness, or pin changed this lap. The count is 9076 lap commits; orbit round 1 (Aries, fire, talent), so the earth-sign verb rule does not apply. Rota lap 9076 falls on row 1, air feels.
obs Option A: drop HEAD from the key for the watched inputs only. A commit that touches only the card or session logs stops moving the digest; HEAD stays recorded beside it as metadata. Cost: the runner contract changes, so the roster witness and the receipt schema move together.
obs Option B: keep HEAD in the key and share only the compile layer, which already shares by content (tools/fixtures/b/shared_build_cache_census.sh reads 819 new files on a miss and 0 from another checkout path). Cost: each ship's --scoped pass still reruns the roster on its own HEAD, so the cold run is not shared.
obs Recommendation: Option A, with a witness that proves a card-only commit leaves the key unchanged and a source-touching commit moves it. It is the smaller contract change, and it is the one that lets one green receipt serve seven checkouts.
obs Not measured this lap. The reading that would settle how much an A-key saves is the count of cold runs whose tree differs from the last green receipt only by HEAD. That count is free: run it from the receipt history, do not trust this line.
rota lap 9076, row 1 -- air feels; the reading surfaced that a hold repeated nine times is a request the ruling has not yet had in a usable form, so the options are written out here rather than a tenth hold.
orbit round 1 Aries -- Commence. Finish one to-do.
file construction/ITINERARY.md why-one-line: the receipt-key YOURS line this log answers; the card itself is not edited this lap, since no work commit landed and its git nib pin stays as Keaton last read it.
file session-logs/date/README-index-20261010.md why-one-line: the shelf row for this log, under the delimiter.
scope the receipt key ruling options, written for Keaton; no code, no witness, no pin touched.
status GREEN for the reading (no instrument changed, path presence confirmed with path_absence_scan, claim board empty); the build itself is held, awaiting Keaton's ruling on the receipt key.
recommend check-in (either) -- Keaton picks Option A or Option B on the receipt key; once picked, the lap builds the chosen contract and its witness in one commit.
